Instead of looking backward, this one scans from left to right and keeps unfinished operators on a stack. Each frame stores the operator and the expressions already found for it. When a frame gets two expressions, it is complete and its postfix text is passed to the frame above it.

This is useful when keeping the original prefix direction makes the code easier to follow.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    struct Frame {
        char op;
        vector<string> parts;
    };

    bool isOperator(char ch) {
        return ch == '+' || ch == '-' || ch == '*' ||
               ch == '/' || ch == '^';
    }

public:
    string prefixToPostfix(const string& s) {
        vector<Frame> frames;
        string answer;

        auto addPart = [&](const string& part) {
            string current = part;

            while (true) {
                if (frames.empty()) {
                    answer = current;
                    return;
                }

                Frame& top = frames.back();
                top.parts.push_back(current);

                if (top.parts.size() < 2) {
                    return;
                }

                current = top.parts[0] + top.parts[1] + top.op;
                frames.pop_back();
            }
        };

        for (char token : s) {
            if (token == ' ') {
                continue;
            }

            if (isOperator(token)) {
                frames.push_back({token, {}});
            } else {
                addPart(string(1, token));
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

For `+AB`, `+` opens a frame, `A` fills its first slot, and `B` fills the second. The frame then produces `AB+`.

The code assumes a valid binary prefix expression. With invalid input, there may be unfinished frames, as is common for platform methods that provide valid test cases.

Time complexity: O(n) for the scan, ignoring repeated copying inside very long partial strings.
Space complexity: O(n) for the frames and stored results.

I used a tree here instead of joining the answer during the first scan. Prefix notation naturally gives the root before its children, so a recursive function can build the tree. After that, an iterative postorder walk visits left child, right child, and finally the operator, which is postfix order.

This uses more objects than a direct stack, but the two steps are easy to check separately.

```python
class Solution:
    class Node:
        def __init__(self, value: str):
            self.value = value
            self.left = None
            self.right = None

    def prefixToPostfix(self, s: str) -> str:
        tokens = [ch for ch in s if ch != " "]
        if not tokens:
            return ""

        operators = {"+", "-", "*", "/", "^"}
        position = 0

        def build_tree():
            nonlocal position
            node = Solution.Node(tokens[position])
            position += 1

            if node.value in operators:
                node.left = build_tree()
                node.right = build_tree()

            return node

        root = build_tree()
        result = []
        stack = [(root, False)]

        while stack:
            node, visited = stack.pop()
            if visited:
                result.append(node.value)
                continue

            stack.append((node, True))
            if node.right is not None:
                stack.append((node.right, False))
            if node.left is not None:
                stack.append((node.left, False))

        return "".join(result)
```

A single operand such as `A` becomes a one-node tree and returns unchanged. For `+*ABC`, the tree is walked as `A B * C +`, giving `AB*C+`.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(n) for the tokens, tree, and traversal stack.

A stack makes the scan fairly direct here. Read the prefix expression from right to left. When a value is found, store it. When an operator appears, remove the next two values, place the operator after them, and push the new string back.

Spaces are ignored, so inputs with or without spaces work the same way.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string prefixToPostfix(const string& s) {
        vector<string> stack;

        auto isOperator = [](char ch) {
            return ch == '+' || ch == '-' || ch == '*' ||
                   ch == '/' || ch == '^';
        };

        for (int i = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            if (s[i] == ' ') {
                continue;
            }

            if (!isOperator(s[i])) {
                stack.push_back(string(1, s[i]));
            } else {
                string left = stack.back();
                stack.pop_back();
                string right = stack.back();
                stack.pop_back();

                stack.push_back(left + right + s[i]);
            }
        }

        return stack.empty() ? "" : stack.back();
    }
};
```

For `*+ABC`, the scan builds `BC+` first, then combines it with `A` to get `ABC+*`.

Time complexity: O(n), where n is the expression length.
Space complexity: O(n) for the stack and partial strings.

This version reads the expression from left to right with a small recursive parser. An operand gives one result immediately. An operator first parses its left expression, then its right expression, and joins both results with the operator at the end.

The index is shared by the recursive calls, so each character is consumed once. Whitespace is skipped before every token.

```java
class Solution {
    private int index;

    public String prefixToPostfix(String s) {
        index = 0;
        return parse(s);
    }

    private String parse(String s) {
        while (index < s.length() && s.charAt(index) == ' ') {
            index++;
        }

        if (index >= s.length()) {
            return "";
        }

        char token = s.charAt(index++);
        if (!isOperator(token)) {
            return String.valueOf(token);
        }

        String left = parse(s);
        String right = parse(s);
        return left + right + token;
    }

    private boolean isOperator(char token) {
        return token == '+' || token == '-' || token == '*' ||
               token == '/' || token == '^';
    }
}
```

For `-A*BC`, the first call sees `-`, then parses `A` and `*BC`. The final result is `ABC*-`.

An empty input returns an empty string. The recursive depth can reach O(n), so a very long expression may use a noticeable call stack.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(n) for recursion and the returned strings.

This solution first connects the prefix tokens into an expression tree. Operators are kept in a stack until both child positions are filled. Once the tree is ready, a postorder traversal prints operands before their operator.

It also handles the one-character edge case without needing a special branch.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    private class Node
    {
        public char Value;
        public Node Left;
        public Node Right;

        public Node(char value)
        {
            Value = value;
        }
    }

    public string PrefixToPostfix(string s)
    {
        Stack<Node> pending = new Stack<Node>();
        Node root = null;

        foreach (char token in s)
        {
            if (token == ' ')
            {
                continue;
            }

            Node node = new Node(token);

            if (pending.Count == 0)
            {
                root = node;
            }
            else
            {
                Node parent = pending.Peek();
                if (parent.Left == null)
                {
                    parent.Left = node;
                }
                else
                {
                    parent.Right = node;
                }
            }

            if (IsOperator(token))
            {
                pending.Push(node);
            }

            while (pending.Count > 0 &&
                   pending.Peek().Left != null &&
                   pending.Peek().Right != null)
            {
                pending.Pop();
            }
        }

        if (root == null)
        {
            return "";
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        WritePostfix(root, result);
        return result.ToString();
    }

    private bool IsOperator(char token)
    {
        return token == '+' || token == '-' || token == '*' ||
               token == '/' || token == '^';
    }

    private void WritePostfix(Node node, StringBuilder result)
    {
        if (node == null)
        {
            return;
        }

        WritePostfix(node.Left, result);
        WritePostfix(node.Right, result);
        result.Append(node.Value);
    }
}
```

For `*+ABC`, the stack connects `*` to `+`, then attaches `A`, `B`, and `C` in order. The postorder walk returns `AB+C*`.

An empty string returns an empty result. The input is not changed.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(n) for the tree, operator stack, and recursion.
